pro-democracy activists deliver a  petition to beijing's liaison office in  hong kong  they want the group of 12 held on the  mainland of china  to be released after more than four  months in detention  there's a secret trial you know people  are not allowed the family members are  not allowed  uh to go on to hear the trial out  and and also they have not met with  their family member  over the 128 days that they are detained  so then no transparency  no visitation by family it's all  harassment tight security surrounded a  court in the southern chinese city of  shenzhen  as 10 of the 12 activists attended the  first day of the trial  they are facing criminal charges  relating to last year's anti-government  protests in hong kong  and one of the accused has been charged  under the national security law  in august they were caught by chinese  authorities trying to flee hong  kong by boat they've now been accused of  illegally entering mainland waters  family members say they've had no  contact since their arrest  and were denied permission to attend  monday's court hearing in shenzhen  they now fear for their safety  i beg the courts to give the verdict as  soon as possible  i want to see my son again very much  the families of the 12 have been in  great agony throughout their detention  they are now only asking for the safety  of the children and their earliest  return to hong kong  the united states has demanded the  accused be returned to hong kong to face  trial but china has rejected that  request  accusing the us of interfering in their  domestic affairs  hong kong's chief executive says it's a  matter for the mainland  if these hong kong residents were  arrested  for bridging mainland offenses  then they have to be dealt with  according to  the mainland laws in accordance with the  jurisdiction  before any other things could happen  another nine people  in hong kong have been arrested  suspected of helping the group  flee the city in august they've been  accused of arranging transport  and the police here haven't ruled out  making more arrests  sarah clark al jazeera hong kong
 

